    "evidence_text": "While successful, this approach is expensive in terms of hardware, runtime and CO_2 emissions.\n\nWhen applying PTLMs to specific domains, it can be useful to domain-adapt them.\n\nDomain adaptation of PTLMs\n\nhas typically been achieved by pretraining on target-domain text.\n\nThe PTLM thus gains domain-specific lexical knowledge in the form of additional word vectors, but its deeper layers remain unchanged.\n\nIn Section SECREF4, we use the proposed method to domain-adapt BERT on PubMed+PMC\n\nusually replaced with a randomly initialized task-specific layer.\n\nIn this paper, we focus on BioBERT, as its domain adaptation corpora are publicly available.\n\nIn this paper, we use Word2Vec  to train word vectors.\n\nWe will denote the Word2Vec lookup function as\n\nWe refer to the resulting model as GreenCovidSQuADBERT.\n\ndata and align them with the wordpiece vector space of a general-domain PTLM.\n\nThen, we train CBOW Word2Vec with negative sampling.\n\nFor detailed dataset statistics, see .\n\nWe cut all sentences into chunks of 30 or fewer whitespace-tokenized words (without splitting inside labeled spans).\n\nLike , we finetune on the concatenation of the training and development set.\n\nAll finetuning runs were done on a GeForce Titan X GPU (12GB).\n\nAt inference time, we gather the output logits of word-initial wordpieces only.\n\nInexpensive Domain Adaptation of Pretrained Language Models\n\nEvery question is associated with one of 98 research papers (contexts).\n\nWe treat the entire dataset as a test set.\n\nNote that there are some important differences between the dataset and SQuAD, which make the task challenging: The contexts are full documents rather than single paragraphs.\n\nThus, the correct answer may appear several times, often with slightly different wordings.",
